1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,600
Welcome to Agentic Knowledge Draft Construction, built in partnership with Neo4j.

2
00:00:05,100 --> 00:00:11,780
In this course, you design a multi-agent system that transforms your structured and unstructured data into a knowledge draft.

3
00:00:12,180 --> 00:00:15,680
I found knowledge drafts really useful for high-stakes applications,

4
00:00:16,000 --> 00:00:20,120
where accuracy of the information we store and retrieve is important.

5
00:00:20,500 --> 00:00:25,240
This course will teach you why, and also give you a great set of tools for building these knowledge drafts.

6
00:00:25,900 --> 00:00:29,280
I'm delighted that the instructor for this course is Andres Colleger,

7
00:00:29,280 --> 00:00:33,680
who is a Developer Evangelist for Genes of AI at Neo4j.

8
00:00:34,380 --> 00:00:38,480
Thanks, Andrew. I'm excited to be back here and to work with you on this course.

9
00:00:39,080 --> 00:00:44,380
Similar to RAG, a knowledge draft system splits your text documents into chunks

10
00:00:44,380 --> 00:00:46,460
and stores them in the vector database.

11
00:00:46,880 --> 00:00:51,180
But in addition to that, it places the chunks into a graph structure,

12
00:00:51,400 --> 00:00:55,000
which a chunk is linked to entities extracted from the chunks.

13
00:00:55,360 --> 00:00:58,180
For example, a text chunk from a product review

14
00:00:58,180 --> 00:01:03,020
can contain entities such as product orders or delivery issues or product defects.

15
00:01:03,280 --> 00:01:06,860
To build a knowledge draft, you extract the relevant entities from the chunks,

16
00:01:07,040 --> 00:01:10,860
then connect the chunks and the entities in the graph through edges.

17
00:01:11,600 --> 00:01:14,540
Here, each edge represents a relationship. For example,

18
00:01:15,060 --> 00:01:17,760
maybe it represents that this chunk mentioned a certain product,

19
00:01:17,760 --> 00:01:24,320
or that that product has an issue. The entities are retrieved alongside the relevant chunks

20
00:01:24,320 --> 00:01:30,960
to provide more relevant context to the LLM to generate more precise and accurate answers.

21
00:01:31,500 --> 00:01:36,820
You can also connect this type of graph to another graph containing additional information

22
00:01:36,820 --> 00:01:40,440
extracted from structured data such as CSE files.

23
00:01:41,180 --> 00:01:45,300
In this course, Andreas will walk you through how to build a multi-agent system

24
00:01:45,300 --> 00:01:48,760
that helps you do all this work of constructing this type of knowledge graph.

25
00:01:48,960 --> 00:01:52,880
To transform your structured and unstructured data into a knowledge graph,

26
00:01:53,180 --> 00:01:55,560
you first need to decide on the graph schema,

27
00:01:56,080 --> 00:01:59,720
meaning what types of entities or nodes you can extract from the data

28
00:01:59,720 --> 00:02:04,480
and what relationships exist between them. Once you have the schema defined,

29
00:02:04,840 --> 00:02:08,080
you can construct the actual graph and store it in a graph database.

30
00:02:08,400 --> 00:02:11,240
Instead of mainly looking at your data to find the graph schema,

31
00:02:11,600 --> 00:02:15,580
you'll design a multi-agent system for doing that using Google's ADK,

32
00:02:15,640 --> 00:02:20,080
or Agent Development Kit. After you learn the basic syntax with ADK,

33
00:02:20,420 --> 00:02:25,440
you'll design your system one agent at a time. The first agent will converse with you

34
00:02:25,440 --> 00:02:27,920
to extract the goal and type of graph you want to build.

35
00:02:27,920 --> 00:02:33,220
Then given that goal, a group of agents will specialize in extracting the entities

36
00:02:33,220 --> 00:02:35,560
and relationships from your structured data,

37
00:02:36,080 --> 00:02:38,980
and another group of agents will handle your unstructured data.

38
00:02:39,600 --> 00:02:42,700
Finally, the final set of agents will connect the two models

39
00:02:42,700 --> 00:02:44,380
and construct the graph accordingly.

40
00:02:44,960 --> 00:02:47,320
Many people have worked to create this course.

41
00:02:47,540 --> 00:02:52,240
I'd like to thank Premier4J, Martin O'Hanlon, and Adam Cowley.

42
00:02:52,580 --> 00:02:56,300
From deeplearning.ai, Christopher Policastro and Hara Salami

43
00:02:56,300 --> 00:02:59,120
also contributed to this course. In the first lesson,

44
00:02:59,240 --> 00:03:02,560
you'll learn more about the underlying structure of knowledge drafts

45
00:03:02,560 --> 00:03:07,020
and how you can build one and use it to identify root causes of product issues.

46
00:03:07,020 --> 00:03:09,720
Sounds great. Let's get started.

47
00:03:10,040 --> 00:03:10,080
Let's get started.

48
00:03:10,080 --> 00:03:10,720
Let's dat!

